{
  "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
  "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument this morning in Case Number 16-1140, the National Institute of Family and Life Advocates versus Becerra."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey B. Wall",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The First Amendment allows states to require truthful, factual disclosures about one's own goods or services. What the First Amendment does not allow and what California has done is to require pregnancy centers to make disclosures about services they do not provide and that would violate their most deeply held beliefs without any showing by the state that it truly needs to compel speech rather than speak its own message."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JEFFREY B. WALL ON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, IN SUPPORT OF NEITHER PARTY"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Well, if it's the first kind of statute, then why shouldn't this Court take cognizance of the state's other available means to provide messages?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0014",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KLEIN: Well, Your Honor, the state -- the legislature is aware of the shortcomings of other methods, as evidenced by Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Well, but if you're trying to educate a class of -- of persons about their rights, it's -- it's pretty unusual to force a private speaker to do that for you under the First Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0016",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KLEIN: And the answer is no, they're not required to."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Why not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0002-0018",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the language issue wasn't raised at all on preliminary injunction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And so taking it on a narrow and then a broader construction, the narrow construction, as I understood your hypothetical, was a family planning center that didn't actually perform abortions but did all the other services. I believe it would be unconstitutional in a pro-life state to require that center because -- to give a pro-life kind of disclaimer that's -- was required in Pennsylvania because they're not doing anything that relates to the practice of medicine in that context. But taking the broader construction, if we're not going to gerrymander this and say: All doctors who treat pregnant women have to give all the options. If -- if that's the case, doctors who advise to deliver and doctors who advise to get abortions are going to be swept into this requirement. And the political ramifications of that are enormous. We should not politicize the practice of medicine in that way. And the line that Casey drew between performing abortions versus advising about abortions is a constitutionally appropriate line."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1140",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a807148f557f05e3ff00048565039850da3aef64122926e19aa177cfa7158af1",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a807148f557f-0003-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Thank you, Your Honor. I rest. CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted. (Whereupon, at 11:10 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
    {
